---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Container from '../components/Container.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
---

<Layout title="Building with guava-os">
  <Container>
    <article class="py-12 md:py-16">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
        <header class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-sm font-mono text-muted-foreground mb-3">Case study · guava-os</p>
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-serif font-semibold leading-tight">
            A control plane for agents, and the loop I use to build with them
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base md:text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
            guava-os is how I orchestrate parallel AI coding agents. It didn't start as a product — it
            started as a loop I kept repeating by hand until the repetition begged to be codified.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="space-y-12 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed text-foreground/90">
          <section data-reveal>
            <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold mb-4">Chat &amp; plan is the core loop</h2>
            <p class="mb-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Every piece of work starts as a conversation, and the first artifact is never code — it's a
              plan. I describe a goal in plain language, then push on it until it decomposes into scoped,
              verifiable units. This is where I separate <em>what</em> from <em>how</em>, and where I name
              the contracts between pieces before anyone touches a file.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              Planning is the step that compounds. A plan that names its boundaries and its acceptance
              criteria makes the next three steps boring — and boring is the point. The loop is short:
              chat to a plan, plan to dispatched work, work to a gate, gate back to chat. Most of the real
              thinking lives in the first turn and the last one; the middle should run itself.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section data-reveal>
            <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold mb-4">Dispatching work to agents</h2>
            <p class="mb-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Once the plan exists, its units fan out to workers. Each worker gets a self-contained brief:
              the files it owns, the pattern it should follow, and the acceptance it has to meet. Isolation
              is the whole point — agents that never touch the same file can run in parallel without
              colliding. Dependencies become explicit edges, so a unit only starts when its upstream has
              landed.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              What I don't do is hand an agent a blank page and hope. A worker with no context invents
              context, and invented context produces confident, wrong work. So the dispatch step carries
              the shape of the problem, not just the task. The harder the job, the more of my own judgment
              gets folded into the brief before it goes out.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section data-reveal>
            <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold mb-4">Gates and enforcement</h2>
            <p class="mb-4 text-muted-foreground">
              Parallelism is only as safe as the gate that reviews its output. Every change comes back
              through a review that reads the diff and checks it against the plan that spawned it — did the
              work do what the issue asked, and did it respect the conventions it was handed? A gate
              authorizes the merge; the worker that wrote the code doesn't. Nothing lands on a shared
              branch until it passes, and no worker ever merges toward production on its own.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              The gates are what make the loop trustworthy enough to run unattended. Speed without them is
              just a faster way to break a shared branch. With them, I can let a dozen agents run while I
              do the only part a human should: decide what's worth building, and judge whether what came
              back is actually good.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section data-reveal>
            <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold mb-4">Stack notes</h2>
            <p class="mb-4 text-muted-foreground">
              The system is TypeScript end to end — one language from orchestration to worker runtime keeps
              the contract surface small. Work lives in Linear, and its GraphQL API is the source of truth:
              issues are the state of record, comments carry the trail, and statuses drive the gates.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              <Badge variant="secondary">TypeScript</Badge>
              <Badge variant="secondary">Linear GraphQL</Badge>
              <Badge variant="secondary">OMP</Badge>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </Container>
</Layout>
